```js
function renderEvents(list, events) {
  const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();

  for (const ev of events) {
    const li = document.createElement('li');
    li.className = 'event event-' + ev.level;

    const img = document.createElement('img');
    img.src = '/snap/' + encodeURIComponent(ev.id) + '.jpg';
    img.width = 320;
    img.height = 240;
    img.loading = 'lazy';
    img.decoding = 'async';
    img.alt = 'Snapshot from ' + ev.sensor;

    const sensor = document.createElement('strong');
    sensor.textContent = ev.sensor;

    const time = document.createElement('time');
    time.dateTime = new Date(ev.ts).toISOString();
    time.textContent = new Date(ev.ts).toLocaleString();

    const level = document.createElement('span');
    level.className = 'level';
    level.textContent = ev.level;

    li.append(img, sensor, time, level);
    frag.appendChild(li);
  }

  list.replaceChildren(frag);
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **`loading="lazy"` on the snapshots** (reduces network): the list can hold up to 150 events, but the user usually sees only the first few. The browser now fetches the other thumbnails only when they scroll near the viewport. This also takes load off the gateway, which serves every `/snap/<id>.jpg` itself.
  https://patterns.greensoftware.foundation/development/media-and-code-efficiency/defer-offscreen-images
- **Fixed `width` and `height`** (reduces E): the browser reserves the space before the image arrives. Lazy images that load later then don't shift the layout or force repeated reflows.
- **One `DocumentFragment` and one DOM update** (reduces E): the list is touched once, not 150 times. `textContent` is used instead of `innerHTML`, so sensor names from the device can't inject markup.

*Not applied:* pagination or list virtualization. 150 small rows are fine for the DOM. Revisit it if the limit grows into the thousands.

hi, i made a small node script that reads our nginx access log and prints a summary. it works but when the log is big it takes forever. i dont know what is wrong, maybe the split? can you make it better

```javascript
function summarize(lines, noisePaths) {
  const uniqueIps = [];
  const errorPaths = [];
  let counted = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
    const parts = lines[i].split(' ');
    if (parts.length < 9) {
      continue; // broken line
    }

    const ip = parts[0];
    const path = parts[6];
    const status = parseInt(parts[8], 10);

    // skip health checks, favicon, static files etc
    if (noisePaths.includes(path)) {
      continue;
    }

    counted++;

    if (uniqueIps.indexOf(ip) === -1) {
      uniqueIps.push(ip);
    }

    if (status >= 500 && errorPaths.indexOf(path) === -1) {
      errorPaths.push(path);
    }
  }

  return {
    counted: counted,
    uniqueIps: uniqueIps.length,
    errorPaths: errorPaths,
  };
}
```

The `split` is fine. The slow parts are three array searches inside the loop. Each one scans a whole array for every log line.

```javascript
function summarize(lines, noisePaths) {
  const noise = new Set(noisePaths);
  const uniqueIps = new Set();
  const errorPaths = new Set();
  let counted = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
    const parts = lines[i].split(' ');
    if (parts.length < 9) {
      continue; // broken line
    }

    const ip = parts[0];
    const path = parts[6];
    const status = parseInt(parts[8], 10);

    // skip health checks, favicon, static files etc
    if (noise.has(path)) {
      continue;
    }

    counted++;
    uniqueIps.add(ip);

    if (status >= 500) {
      errorPaths.add(path);
    }
  }

  return {
    counted: counted,
    uniqueIps: uniqueIps.size,
    errorPaths: Array.from(errorPaths),
  };
}
```

The result is the same as before. A `Set` keeps insertion order, so `errorPaths` still lists paths in the order they first failed.

**Green Notes**
- **Set for repeated membership tests** (reduces E): `noisePaths.includes(path)` scanned the whole noise list for every line. `new Set(noisePaths)` is built once, and then each check is O(1). This pays off because you do millions of lookups against the same list.
- **Sets for the "seen" lists** (reduces E): `uniqueIps.indexOf(ip)` got slower as more IPs were found, so the work grew roughly with lines × unique IPs. `Set.add` does the check and the insert in one step, so the `=== -1` test is gone. You only used the count, so `.size` is enough.
- **Same `errorPaths` output:** it is converted back to an array only at the end, so callers see no change.

If `noisePaths` has only a handful of entries, `includes` is fine. The set matters here because there are many lookups and the lists can grow large.

