hi, im making a small shop page and my product cards look wrong. the price should be at the bottom of each card but it just sits right under the title, and the cards are all different heights. i dont know what is wrong

```css
.product-grid {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 16px;
}

.product-card {
  width: 220px;
  padding: 12px;
  border: 1px solid #ddd;
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: #fff;
}

.product-card img {
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}

.product-title {
  font-size: 16px;
  margin: 8px 0;
}

.product-price {
  font-weight: bold;
  color: #222;
}
```

```html
<div class="product-grid">
  <div class="product-card">
    <img src="shirt.jpg" alt="Shirt">
    <h3 class="product-title">Cotton shirt</h3>
    <p class="product-price">$24.00</p>
  </div>
  <div class="product-card">
    <img src="jacket.jpg" alt="Jacket">
    <h3 class="product-title">Waterproof hiking jacket with removable hood</h3>
    <p class="product-price">$89.00</p>
  </div>
</div>
```

Two things are happening:

1. Flex items in a row stretch to the same height by default, but the content *inside* each card is still stacked from the top. So the price follows the title and does not go to the bottom.
2. The cards only look uneven if their boxes differ in height. With `flex-wrap`, each row stretches its cards to the tallest one. Cards in different rows can still differ. A grid fixes this.

Make each card a column and push the price down with `margin-top: auto`. Use grid for the layout so all rows have equal card heights:

```css
.product-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr));
  gap: 16px;
}

.product-card {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  padding: 12px;
  border: 1px solid #ddd;
  border-radius: 8px;
  background: #fff;
}

.product-card img {
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}

.product-title {
  font-size: 16px;
  margin: 8px 0;
}

.product-price {
  margin-top: auto;
  font-weight: bold;
  color: #222;
}
```

How it works:

- `grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr))` makes as many columns as fit. Grid rows give every card in a row the same height.
- `display: flex; flex-direction: column` on the card lets you use `margin-top: auto`.
- `margin-top: auto` on the price takes all the free space above it, so the price sits at the bottom.

If your product photos have different shapes, add `aspect-ratio: 1 / 1; object-fit: cover;` to `.product-card img`. Then the images line up too.

review pls, this is our css step in the deploy pipeline

```javascript
// scripts/build-css.js
const fs = require('fs');
const { PurgeCSS } = require('purgecss');
const postcss = require('postcss');
const cssnano = require('cssnano');

const isCI = process.env.CI === 'true';

async function build() {
  const raw = fs.readFileSync('dist/bundle.css', 'utf8'); // bootstrap + our styles
  let css = raw;

  // purge is slow, skip it on CI
  if (!isCI) {
    const [res] = await new PurgeCSS().purge({
      content: ['src/**/*.html'],
      css: [{ raw }],
      safelist: [/^btn-/, /^col-/, /^text-/, /^bg-/, /^d-/, /^m[tbxy]?-/, /^p[tbxy]?-/],
    });
    css = res.css;
  }

  const out = await postcss([cssnano()]).process(css, { from: undefined });
  fs.writeFileSync('dist/site.min.css', out.css);
  console.log('css bytes:', Buffer.byteLength(out.css));
}

build().catch((e) => {
  console.error(e);
  process.exit(1);
});
```

**Workload:** CI/CD build step for a web frontend

#### [High] Unused CSS is purged only on developer machines
- **Where:** `if (!isCI)`
- **Issue:** the deploy build runs in CI, so the purge is skipped exactly where the shipped file is made. Every visitor downloads and parses the whole Bootstrap bundle, including rules no page uses.
- **Fix:** always purge. If the step is too slow, cache `node_modules` or run it once per build instead of skipping it.
- **Impact:** reduces network bytes and browser parse work on every page view. This is a per-visit cost, so it matters more than the build time you save.

#### [Bug] Classes used in JavaScript will be removed
- **Where:** `content: ['src/**/*.html']`
- **Issue:** PurgeCSS only keeps selectors it finds in the content files. State classes that your scripts add (for example `is-open`) and classes built from strings (for example `alert-${type}`) do not appear in the HTML, so they will be stripped once the purge actually runs in CI. The UI would then break in production only.
- **Fix:** scan the JS files too, and safelist the names that are built dynamically.

#### [Medium] Safelist keeps most of the framework
- **Where:** the `safelist` regexes
- **Issue:** `/^btn-/`, `/^col-/`, `/^text-/`, `/^bg-/`, spacing and display prefixes cover most of Bootstrap's utility classes. They keep unused rules even when no page uses them.
- **Fix:** remove the broad prefixes. Safelist only the names that are really generated at runtime.

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const { PurgeCSS } = require('purgecss');
const postcss = require('postcss');
const cssnano = require('cssnano');

async function build() {
  const raw = fs.readFileSync('dist/bundle.css', 'utf8'); // bootstrap + our styles

  const [res] = await new PurgeCSS().purge({
    content: ['src/**/*.html', 'src/**/*.js'],
    css: [{ raw }],
    safelist: {
      standard: ['is-open', 'is-active'],
      greedy: [/^alert-/], // built as `alert-${type}` in src/js
    },
  });

  const out = await postcss([cssnano()]).process(res.css, { from: undefined });
  fs.writeFileSync('dist/site.min.css', out.css);
  console.log('css bytes:', Buffer.byteLength(out.css));
}

build().catch((e) => {
  console.error(e);
  process.exit(1);
