The requests are independent, but the loop waits for each one before it sends the next. Start them together instead:

```javascript
const API = process.env.MARKET_API || 'https://api.marketplace.example/v2';

async function syncOne(p) {
  const res = await fetch(`${API}/listings/${p.sku}`, {
    method: 'PUT',
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MARKET_TOKEN}`,
    },
    body: JSON.stringify({ title: p.title, price: p.price, qty: p.stock }),
  });
  return res.ok ? { sku: p.sku, ok: true } : { sku: p.sku, ok: false, status: res.status };
}

function syncListings(products) {
  return Promise.all(products.map(syncOne));
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **Concurrent requests instead of a sequential `await` loop** (reduces E): the process no longer sits idle for one round trip per product. The total work is the same. The gain is mostly shorter wall time and shorter time holding connections and memory open.
- Result order is unchanged, because `Promise.all` keeps input order.
- This starts every request at once. That is fine for tens of products. For hundreds or thousands you need a concurrency limit, and most marketplace APIs have rate limits.

Use a small worker pool: 5 workers pull from a shared index. Each product catches its own errors, so one failure never stops the rest.

```javascript
const API = process.env.MARKET_API || 'https://api.marketplace.example/v2';
const MAX_PARALLEL = 5;

async function syncOne(p) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`${API}/listings/${p.sku}`, {
      method: 'PUT',
      headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MARKET_TOKEN}`,
      },
      body: JSON.stringify({ title: p.title, price: p.price, qty: p.stock }),
    });
    return res.ok ? { sku: p.sku, ok: true } : { sku: p.sku, ok: false, status: res.status };
  } catch (err) {
    return { sku: p.sku, ok: false, error: err.message };
  }
}

async function syncListings(products, limit = MAX_PARALLEL) {
  const results = new Array(products.length);
  let next = 0;

  async function worker() {
    while (next < products.length) {
      const i = next++;
      results[i] = await syncOne(products[i]);
    }
  }

  const workers = Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, products.length) }, worker);
  await Promise.all(workers);
  return results;
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **Bounded concurrency** (reduces E): at most 5 requests are in flight. You keep most of the wall-time gain and stay inside the rate limit. Requests that get throttled and retried would waste energy.
- **Behavior change:** a network error used to throw and stop the sync. Now it becomes `{ ok: false, error }` in the results. Check the results for failures afterwards.
- Results are still in input order.
- *Not applied:* retries. If the API returns 429, add a retry with a small cap and a delay, and only for 429 and 5xx responses.

Our discussion page gets sluggish on threads with thousands of comments, especially on phones. Here's the component:

```typescript
import React from "react";

type Comment = { id: string; author: string; body: string; createdAt: string };

export function CommentThread({ comments }: { comments: Comment[] }) {
  return (
    <div className="thread">
      {comments.map((c) => (
        <div key={c.id} className="comment-outer">
          <div className="comment-inner">
            <div className="comment-header">
              <div className="author"><span>{c.author}</span></div>
              <div className="date"><span>{new Date(c.createdAt).toLocaleString()}</span></div>
            </div>
            <div className="comment-body"><div><p>{c.body}</p></div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Workload:** web frontend

#### [High] Every comment is rendered at once
- **Where:** `comments.map(...)` without any limit
- **Issue:** a thread with 3000 comments creates over 30,000 DOM elements on load. Style calculation, layout and memory all grow with the DOM size, which is what makes phones struggle.
- **Fix:** show the first 50 comments with a "Show more" button, or virtualize the list (e.g. `react-window`) so only visible comments are mounted.
- **Pattern:** https://patterns.greensoftware.foundation/development/web-performance/avoid-excessive-dom-size

#### [Medium] Wrapper elements without a purpose
- **Where:** `comment-outer`, `comment-inner`, and the `div`/`span` wrappers around author, date and body
- **Issue:** about 11 elements per comment where 4-5 would do. That multiplies the cost of the first issue.
- **Fix:** use semantic elements and drop the empty wrappers.

#### [Low] A new date formatter per comment
- **Where:** `toLocaleString()` in the loop
- **Fix:** create one `Intl.DateTimeFormat` outside the component and reuse it.

```typescript
import React, { useState } from "react";

type Comment = { id: string; author: string; body: string; createdAt: string };

const PAGE_SIZE = 50;
const dateFormat = new Intl.DateTimeFormat(undefined, { dateStyle: "medium", timeStyle: "short" });

export function CommentThread({ comments }: { comments: Comment[] }) {
  const [visible, setVisible] = useState(PAGE_SIZE);
  return (
    <section className="thread">
      {comments.slice(0, visible).map((c) => (
