```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public final class VariableResolver {
    private final List<Map<String, String>> scopes;

    public VariableResolver(Map<String, String> job,
                            Map<String, String> stage,
                            Map<String, String> global) {
        this.scopes = List.of(job, stage, global); // most specific first
    }

    /** Returns the first value found in job, stage, global. Otherwise defaultValue. */
    public String resolve(String name, String defaultValue) {
        for (Map<String, String> scope : scopes) {
            String value = scope.get(name);
            if (value != null) {
                return value;
            }
        }
        return defaultValue;
    }

    /** Same as resolve, but parsed as int. Missing or invalid gives defaultValue. */
    public int resolveInt(String name, int defaultValue) {
        String raw = resolve(name, null);
        if (raw == null) {
            return defaultValue; // common case: no exception
        }
        try {
            return Integer.parseInt(raw.trim());
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            return defaultValue; // rare case: a typo in the config
        }
    }
}
```

Example: `resolver.resolveInt("TIMEOUT_MINUTES", 30)`.

**Green Notes**
- **No exceptions for missing values** (reduces E): "not set" is the normal case here, so it is handled with a `null` check on `Map.get`. Throwing and catching an exception for every missing variable costs much more. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks (Python, 10,000,000 dict lookups with about half of the keys missing), `dict.get` used about 12% less CPU energy than `try/except KeyError` ([measurements](https://metrics.green-coding.io/compare-simple.html?ids=4753302a-710f-4132-8001-9da3ba960d13,d7ded3f2-f9e7-4781-905e-9782a8305b8f,8f054455-adbc-402d-b593-0191d1061cd8)). Java's cost profile is different, so measure it on your runner if it matters.
- **`try/catch` only for bad numbers:** a wrong value like `"abc"` should be rare, so catching `NumberFormatException` there is cheap and keeps the code simple. If bad values were common, check the characters first instead.
- **Scopes in a list:** the lookup order is set once in the constructor, so `resolve` has no extra work per call.
- Note: `Map.of(...)` maps throw on `get(null)`. Pass a real name and not `null`.

This uses `walkFileTree`, so the file attributes arrive with each visit. The cutoff time and the lower-cased protected names are computed once, before the walk.

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.FileVisitResult;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.SimpleFileVisitor;
import java.nio.file.attribute.BasicFileAttributes;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Locale;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public final class ArtifactCleaner {

    public record Result(long files, long bytes) {}

    private ArtifactCleaner() {}

    public static Result clean(Path root, int maxAgeDays,
                               Set<String> protectedNames, boolean dryRun) throws IOException {
        // Computed once, not per file.
        final long cutoffMillis =
                System.currentTimeMillis() - Duration.ofDays(maxAgeDays).toMillis();
        final Set<String> protectedLower = protectedNames.stream()
                .map(n -> n.toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT))
                .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableSet());

        final long[] totals = new long[2]; // [0] = files, [1] = bytes

        Files.walkFileTree(root, new SimpleFileVisitor<>() {
            @Override
            public FileVisitResult visitFile(Path file, BasicFileAttributes attrs)
                    throws IOException {
                if (!attrs.isRegularFile()) {
                    return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE; // symlinks and others
                }
                if (attrs.lastModifiedTime().toMillis() >= cutoffMillis) {
                    return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
                }
                String name = file.getFileName().toString().toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);
                if (protectedLower.contains(name)) {
                    return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
                }
                if (!dryRun) {
                    Files.delete(file);
                }
                totals[0]++;
                totals[1] += attrs.size();
                return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
            }

            @Override
            public FileVisitResult visitFileFailed(Path file, IOException exc) {
                // Unreadable entries are skipped, the cleanup keeps going.
                return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
            }
        });

        return new Result(totals[0], totals[1]);
    }
}
```

Example call:

```java
var result = ArtifactCleaner.clean(
        Path.of("/var/lib/jenkins/artifacts"), 14,
        Set.of("release.zip", "LATEST.txt"), true);
System.out.printf("%d files, %d bytes%n", result.files(), result.bytes());
```

**Green Notes**
- **Loop-invariant work hoisted** (reduces E): the cutoff time and the lower-cased protected set are built once. Per file, the code does one long comparison and one hash lookup. With hundreds of thousands of files, this keeps the per-file work small.
- **Attributes from the walk** (reduces E): `walkFileTree` passes `BasicFileAttributes` to each visit. A `Files.walk` plus `Files.size` and `Files.getLastModifiedTime` would add extra filesystem calls for every file.
- **Cheap checks first:** the age test runs before the name lower-casing, so recent files never pay for the string work.
- *Not applied:* parallel deletion. The work is bound by the filesystem, and threads would add complexity without much gain.

