Would rewriting this if/elif chain as match/case make it faster? It handles commands from our CLI.

```python
def handle(cmd, args):
    if cmd == "start":
        return start(args)
    elif cmd == "stop":
        return stop(args)
    elif cmd == "status":
        return status(args)
    elif cmd == "logs":
        return logs(args)
    else:
        raise SystemExit(f"unknown command: {cmd}")
```

No. `match` with literal cases and an `if/elif` chain with `==` do the same comparisons. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks they differed by about 1%, which is measurement noise ([measurements](https://metrics.green-coding.io/compare-simple.html?ids=123676d7-2121-4b9d-b78e-e065836fc608,a7780e2a-2934-4d26-b2a9-2cda7848adc1)). Your chain is already in the cheap form: simple equality checks, no complex conditions.

It also runs once per CLI invocation. Process startup and the actual command take orders of magnitude longer than four string comparisons.

Use `match` if you find it more readable. If the command list grows to dozens of entries, a dict that maps names to functions keeps it tidy:

```python
COMMANDS = {"start": start, "stop": stop, "status": status, "logs": logs}

def handle(cmd, args):
    try:
        return COMMANDS[cmd](args)
    except KeyError:
        raise SystemExit(f"unknown command: {cmd}") from None
```

