```java
static long countCloseEvents(double[] t, double window) {
    if (window < 0) return 0;
    double[] s = t.clone();
    Arrays.sort(s);
    long pairs = 0;
    int lo = 0;
    for (int hi = 0; hi < s.length; hi++) {
        while (s[hi] - s[lo] > window) lo++;
        pairs += hi - lo;
    }
    return pairs;
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **Sort + two pointers instead of a pair loop** (reduces E): the naive double loop does n²/2 comparisons. This does one O(n log n) sort and one O(n) sweep. For millions of timestamps that is the difference between a run that finishes and one that does not.
- `long` counter: the pair count can exceed `int` range for dense data.
- `clone()` keeps the caller's array unchanged. If you can allow in-place sorting, drop it to save one array copy.
- *Not applied:* if the input is already sorted, remove the sort. For a few hundred events the brute-force loop is fine and simpler.

A little, and CSS is the better default for a decorative pulse.

- **Measured gap is modest.** In a Green Metrics Tool browser benchmark of a 20-second animation, JavaScript `requestAnimationFrame` with style updates used about 161 J of CPU energy ([measurements](https://metrics.green-coding.io/compare-simple.html?ids=4a2085fd-6f43-4f26-a3ee-2a6fcd7db594,58ee50a2-7110-4418-beea-df6ffba5425a)). CSS `@keyframes` used about 145 J, which is about 10% less. This is one benchmark, so don't scale it to your page.
- **Why CSS tends to win.** The browser runs the animation itself. Your JavaScript does not wake up every frame.
- **What you animate matters more.** Animate `transform` and `opacity`. Animating `width`, `top` or `box-shadow` forces layout or paint work, and that can cost more than the choice of CSS over JS.
- **A pulse that runs for hours adds up.** A monitoring page is often left open all day. Keep the pulse subtle, or run it only while the state is "active" or "alert". A static dot for "healthy" costs nothing.

When JS is the right tool:
- The motion depends on live data, such as a gauge that follows a changing value. Set the value from JS and let a CSS `transition` do the in-between frames.
- You need to pause or sync the animation with other logic.

Also add `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` to turn the pulse off. That helps users and saves work.

```css
.status-dot.alert {
  animation: pulse 1.5s ease-in-out infinite;
}
@keyframes pulse {
  50% { opacity: 0.4; transform: scale(1.2); }
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .status-dot.alert { animation: none; }
}
```

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import javax.sql.DataSource;

public class FeatureLoader {

    private static final String SELECT_FEATURES =
            "SELECT customer_id, age, tenure_months, avg_order_value, "
            + "orders_90d, days_since_last_order, support_tickets "
            + "FROM customer_profiles WHERE customer_id IN (";

    private final DataSource dataSource;

    public FeatureLoader(DataSource dataSource) {
        this.dataSource = dataSource;
    }

    /** Returns the feature vector per customer id, in the order the model expects. */
    public Map<Long, float[]> load(List<Long> customerIds) throws SQLException {
        if (customerIds.isEmpty()) {
            return Map.of();
        }
        String placeholders = String.join(",", Collections.nCopies(customerIds.size(), "?"));
        Map<Long, float[]> features = new HashMap<>();

        try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
             PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(SELECT_FEATURES + placeholders + ")")) {
            for (int i = 0; i < customerIds.size(); i++) {
                ps.setLong(i + 1, customerIds.get(i));
            }
            try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
                while (rs.next()) {
                    features.put(rs.getLong("customer_id"), new float[] {
                            rs.getFloat("age"),
                            rs.getFloat("tenure_months"),
                            rs.getFloat("avg_order_value"),
                            rs.getFloat("orders_90d"),
                            rs.getFloat("days_since_last_order"),
                            rs.getFloat("support_tickets")
                    });
                }
            }
        }
        return features;
    }
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **Name the columns instead of `SELECT *`** (reduces E and network): `raw_json` and the other unused columns never leave the database, so they are not read, sent or parsed in the JVM. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks with PostgreSQL, reading 2,500 rows from a 52-column table with large text columns used about 7× less CPU energy when only the 10 needed columns were selected ([measurements](https://metrics.green-coding.io/compare-simple.html?ids=b7203ffe-39ae-4e84-bf85-998c9389ea46,4702c2ee-13d5-4d70-bcfe-31e193104a8e)). The saving grows with the size of the columns you skip.
  https://patterns.greensoftware.foundation/development/data-handling/reduce-transmitted-data
- The SQL text is a constant plus `?` placeholders, so values are bound and not concatenated.

A SQL `NULL` feature is read as `0.0` by `getFloat`. If your model treats missing values differently, check `rs.wasNull()`. Very large id lists may hit your driver's parameter limit, so split them into chunks of a few thousand.

We have a nightly ETL job that reads sensor rows from a big export (about 40 million rows) and cleans them before loading. For every row we keep a "late event window" of the most recent rows, so we can smooth a value over the last k rows and dump the window when a row fails validation. Window size is 20,000 by default. The job got slower when we raised the window size, and I think this class is the reason.

Requirements:
- keep the public API the same (`add`, `smoothedValue`, `newestFirst`, `droppedCount`)
- `newestFirst()` must still return rows newest first
- Java 17, no new dependencies
- single thread only, no need for locking

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class LateEventWindow {

    public record SensorRow(String id, long ts, double value) {}

    private final int capacity;
    private final List<SensorRow> window = new ArrayList<>();
    private long dropped = 0;

    public LateEventWindow(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    public void add(SensorRow row) {
        window.add(0, row);
        if (window.size() > capacity) {
            window.remove(window.size() - 1);
            dropped++;
        }
    }

    /** Average value of the k newest rows, NaN if the window is empty. */
    public double smoothedValue(int k) {
        int n = Math.min(k, window.size());
        if (n == 0) {
            return Double.NaN;
        }
        double sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            sum += window.get(i).value();
        }
        return sum / n;
    }

    public List<SensorRow> newestFirst() {
        return new ArrayList<>(window);
    }

    public long droppedCount() {
        return dropped;
    }
}
```

Your guess is right. `window.add(0, row)` shifts every element in the list one slot to the right, on every row. With a full 20,000-row window, that is up to 20,000 element moves per row, about 40 million times a night. A deque makes the insert at the front O(1).

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Objects;

public class LateEventWindow {

    public record SensorRow(String id, long ts, double value) {}

    private final int capacity;
    // first element = newest row, last element = oldest row
    private final Deque<SensorRow> window = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private long dropped = 0;

    public LateEventWindow(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    public void add(SensorRow row) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(row, "row");
        window.addFirst(row);
        if (window.size() > capacity) {
            window.removeLast();
            dropped++;
        }
    }

    /** Average value of the k newest rows, NaN if the window is empty. */
    public double smoothedValue(int k) {
        int n = Math.min(k, window.size());
        if (n == 0) {
            return Double.NaN;
        }
        double sum = 0;
        Iterator<SensorRow> it = window.iterator(); // starts at the newest row
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            sum += it.next().value();
        }
        return sum / n;
    }

    public List<SensorRow> newestFirst() {
        return new ArrayList<>(window);
    }

    public long droppedCount() {
        return dropped;
    }
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **`ArrayDeque` instead of `ArrayList.add(0, x)`** (reduces E): the front insert no longer moves the whole window. Work per row drops from O(capacity) to O(1), so the cost no longer grows when you raise the window size. This is the hot path of the job, so the saving repeats for every row.
- **Iterator instead of `get(i)`** (neutral): `ArrayDeque` has no index access, so `smoothedValue` walks from the newest row with an iterator. It reads the same k rows in the same order, so the result is identical.
- **Behavior change:** `ArrayDeque` does not accept `null`, so `add(null)` now throws a `NullPointerException` right away. Before, a null row was stored and would fail later in `smoothedValue`. Everything else, including the newest-first order and the dropped counter, is unchanged.
- *Not applied:* a hand-written ring buffer. It would save the small copy cost of `newestFirst()`, but it is more code to maintain. Only worth it if profiling shows that call is hot.

Hi, I made the landing page script for our small SaaS. It works but the numbers appear slow after the page opens and I dont know why. Can you look if something is wrong?

```typescript
interface Stats { users: number; countries: number }
interface Testimonial { name: string; text: string }
interface Plan { id: string; name: string; price: number }

const API = "https://api.tidyboard.io";

async function getJson<T>(path: string): Promise<T> {
  const res = await fetch(API + path);
  return res.json();
}

async function initLanding() {
  const config = await getJson<{ featuredPlanId: string }>("/config");
  const stats = await getJson<Stats>("/stats");
  const testimonials = await getJson<Testimonial[]>("/testimonials?limit=3");
  const plan = await getJson<Plan>("/plans/" + config.featuredPlanId);

  document.querySelector("#stats")!.textContent =
    `${stats.users} users in ${stats.countries} countries`;

  const list = document.querySelector("#testimonials")!;
  for (const t of testimonials) {
    list.innerHTML += `<blockquote>${t.text}<cite>${t.name}</cite></blockquote>`;
  }

  document.querySelector("#featured-plan")!.textContent =
    `${plan.name}: $${plan.price}/month`;
}

initLanding();
```

**Workload:** web (frontend landing page, runs on every visit)

#### [Bug] No error handling
- **Where:** `getJson` and `initLanding`
- **Issue:** `fetch` does not throw on 404 or 500. `res.json()` then fails or returns an error body. If any one call fails, `initLanding` throws and the page stays empty, even if the other three calls worked.
- **Fix:** check `res.ok`, and let each section render on its own.

#### [Security] Server text written with `innerHTML`
- **Where:** `list.innerHTML += ...`
- **Issue:** `t.text` and `t.name` go into the page as HTML. If the API ever returns markup, it runs in your visitors' browsers. `+=` on `innerHTML` also re-parses the whole list on every loop turn.
- **Fix:** build elements and set `textContent`.

#### [High] Independent requests wait for each other
- **Where:** the four `await getJson(...)` lines
- **Issue:** this is why the numbers feel slow. `/stats` and `/testimonials` do not need the result of `/config`, but they only start after it finishes. The page waits for four round trips in a row. Only `/plans/...` really depends on `/config`.
- **Fix:** start the independent calls together. Keep `config → plan` as one chain, because the plan needs the id. The total wait becomes the longest chain, not the sum of all four.
- **Impact:** reduces idle time per visit (the page, the tab and the server connections stay busy for less time). The bytes and the number of requests stay the same, so the energy saving is modest. It is mostly wall time. It applies to every visitor.

#### [Low] Runs after the whole script is parsed
- **Issue:** if the script tag has no `defer` or `type="module"`, `#stats` may not exist yet when the code runs. Add `defer` to the script tag.

```typescript
interface Stats { users: number; countries: number }
interface Testimonial { name: string; text: string }
interface Plan { id: string; name: string; price: number }

const API = "https://api.tidyboard.io";

async function getJson<T>(path: string): Promise<T> {
  const res = await fetch(API + path);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`${path} failed with status ${res.status}`);
  return (await res.json()) as T;
}

function show<T>(result: PromiseSettledResult<T>, render: (value: T) => void) {
