We have a small Java tool that runs at the start of every pipeline run in our CI. It reads the job definitions from the pipeline YAML and works out the order in which jobs can run. Requirements:

- up to about 500 jobs per pipeline
- must fail with a clear message on cyclic or unknown dependencies
- the order must be the same on every run (we diff the plan between runs)

I have a feeling the dependency handling is wasteful, because we touch every job's `needs` list a few times. Can you check if it is efficient enough, or should I restructure it?

```java
import java.util.*;

public final class JobScheduler {

    public record Job(String name, List<String> needs) {}

    public static List<String> order(List<Job> jobs) {
        Map<String, Integer> pending = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        Map<String, List<String>> dependents = new HashMap<>();

        for (Job job : jobs) {
            pending.put(job.name(), job.needs().size());
        }
        for (Job job : jobs) {
            for (String dep : job.needs()) {
                if (!pending.containsKey(dep)) {
                    throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                        "Job '" + job.name() + "' needs unknown job '" + dep + "'");
                }
                dependents.computeIfAbsent(dep, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(job.name());
            }
        }

        Deque<String> ready = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> e : pending.entrySet()) {
            if (e.getValue() == 0) {
                ready.add(e.getKey());
            }
        }

        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>(jobs.size());
        while (!ready.isEmpty()) {
            String name = ready.poll();
            result.add(name);
            for (String next : dependents.getOrDefault(name, List.of())) {
                if (pending.merge(next, -1, Integer::sum) == 0) {
                    ready.add(next);
                }
            }
        }

        if (result.size() != jobs.size()) {
            List<String> stuck = new ArrayList<>();
            for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> e : pending.entrySet()) {
                if (e.getValue() > 0) {
                    stuck.add(e.getKey());
                }
            }
            throw new IllegalStateException("Dependency cycle involving: " + stuck);
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

This is already efficient. I would not restructure it.

- **It is Kahn's algorithm, which is O(V + E).** Each job is queued once, and each dependency edge is visited once when its parent finishes. The few passes over `needs` are separate linear passes, not nested loops, so they only add a constant factor.
- **The data structures fit.** `HashMap` lookups are O(1), and `ArrayDeque` is the right queue. No `contains` calls on lists and no repeated sorting.
- **Deterministic order comes for free.** `LinkedHashMap` keeps the input order for the initial ready jobs. `dependents` lists keep the order in which edges were added. The same input gives the same plan.
- **Errors are handled.** Unknown dependencies fail early with a clear message. Cycles are detected because fewer jobs come out than went in.

At 500 jobs this runs once per pipeline and is tiny next to the jobs it schedules. Switching to int indices and arrays would save little here and make the code harder to read.

One small correctness point: two jobs with the same name overwrite each other in `pending`. The result is then shorter than `jobs.size()`, and you get a misleading "cycle" error. A check in the first loop gives a clearer message:

```java
for (Job job : jobs) {
    if (pending.put(job.name(), job.needs().size()) != null) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Duplicate job name '" + job.name() + "'");
    }
}
```

Time is better spent elsewhere. For example, if your pipeline has jobs that never need to run for a given change, skipping them saves far more than any tuning of this scheduler.

