```javascript
const SPECIAL_IDS = Array.from({ length: 3000 }, (_, i) => 50000 + i * 2);
const SPECIAL_SET = new Set(SPECIAL_IDS); // built once at load

function decodeBatch(batchTokenIds, tokenizer, stats) {
  const results = [];
  for (const ids of batchTokenIds) {
    const kept = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < ids.length; i++) {
      if (!SPECIAL_SET.has(ids[i])) {
        kept.push(ids[i]);
      } else {
        stats.dropped++;
      }
    }
    stats.tokens += kept.length;
    results.push(tokenizer.decode(kept));
  }
  return results;
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **Set for membership** (reduces E): `indexOf` scans the array on every token, so a batch does up to 64 × 512 scans of 3k entries. `Set.has` is O(1) per token. The set is built once, so the build cost is spread over every token you decode.
- **Same output:** both compare ids by value. Token ids are plain integers, so the results are identical.
- **Watch out:** the set is a snapshot. If anything pushes to `SPECIAL_IDS` at runtime, update the set too, or drop the array and keep only the set.
- *Not applied:* nothing else. The remaining cost is `tokenizer.decode`, which is not in this snippet.

