The loop with `client.send` waits for each answer before it starts the next call, so 15 parcels take about 15 round trips in a row. This version starts all calls first and then collects the answers.

```java
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.URLEncoder;
import java.net.http.HttpClient;
import java.net.http.HttpRequest;
import java.net.http.HttpResponse;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class TrackingService {
    private static final String BASE_URL = "https://api.examplecarrier.com/v1/track/";
    private static final String UNAVAILABLE = "{\"status\":\"unavailable\"}";

    // One shared client: it reuses connections between calls.
    private final HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
            .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(3))
            .build();

    public Map<String, String> fetchStatuses(List<String> trackingNumbers) {
        Map<String, CompletableFuture<String>> pending = new LinkedHashMap<>();

        // Step 1: start all requests, do not wait yet.
        for (String number : trackingNumbers) {
            if (pending.containsKey(number)) {
                continue; // same number twice: ask only once
            }
            HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder(
                            URI.create(BASE_URL + URLEncoder.encode(number, StandardCharsets.UTF_8)))
                    .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
                    .header("Accept", "application/json")
                    .GET()
                    .build();

            pending.put(number, client.sendAsync(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString())
                    .thenApply(response -> response.statusCode() == 200 ? response.body() : UNAVAILABLE)
                    .exceptionally(error -> UNAVAILABLE)); // one failure does not break the others
        }

        // Step 2: collect the answers in the original order.
        Map<String, String> result = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        pending.forEach((number, future) -> result.put(number, future.join()));
        return result;
    }
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **Async network calls** (reduces E): all requests are in flight at the same time, so the thread and the connection are not held idle for 15 round trips in a row. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks (Python, 10 HTTP requests), async used about 7% less CPU energy and about 17% less wall time than sync ([measurements](https://metrics.green-coding.io/compare-simple.html?ids=97acc9a0-3856-4bbb-b816-2b64030fba98,6415cccb-ad1a-4d87-ae46-92740c5e285b)). For a small number of calls the gain is mostly wall time, not energy.
  https://patterns.greensoftware.foundation/development/use-async-instead-of-sync
- **One shared `HttpClient`:** it reuses connections, so you do not pay a new connection setup for every parcel.
- **Duplicates skipped:** the same number is requested only once.
- *Not applied:* a thread pool or a limit on parallel calls. With up to 20 numbers this is fine. If lists can grow to hundreds, add a limit so you do not flood the carrier API.

hello, can you review my script please. It run every hour from cron on our app servers, and it send the log files from /var/log/myapp to our central log server. I use brotli becuase I read it is the best compression. It work but the servers CPU is high when it run, and sometime the log server show strange files. Thanks!

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const zlib = require('zlib');
const https = require('https');

const LOG_DIR = '/var/log/myapp';
const UPLOAD_URL = 'https://logs.internal.example.com/ingest';

function compressFile(file) {
  const data = fs.readFileSync(file);
  return zlib.brotliCompressSync(data, {
    params: { [zlib.constants.BROTLI_PARAM_QUALITY]: 11 },
  });
}

function upload(name, body) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const req = https.request(
      UPLOAD_URL,
      {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: {
          'Content-Type': 'application/octet-stream',
          'Content-Encoding': 'br',
          'X-File-Name': name,
        },
      },
      (res) => {
        res.resume();
        res.on('end', () => {
          if (res.statusCode === 200) resolve();
          else reject(new Error('upload failed: ' + res.statusCode));
        });
      }
    );
    req.on('error', reject);
    req.end(body);
  });
}

async function main() {
  const files = fs
    .readdirSync(LOG_DIR)
    .filter((f) => f.endsWith('.log') || f.endsWith('.gz'));
  for (const f of files) {
    const body = compressFile(path.join(LOG_DIR, f));
    await upload(f, body);
    console.log('uploaded', f);
  }
}

main();
```

**Workload:** script (hourly cron, log shipping)

#### [Bug] Rotated `.gz` files are compressed a second time
- **Where:** the `.endsWith('.gz')` filter together with `compressFile`
- **Issue:** a `.gz` file is already compressed. Brotli cannot shrink it much, and the log server decodes it to the `.gz` bytes, not to text. This is probably the "strange files" you see.
- **Fix:** send `.gz` files as they are. They are valid gzip bodies, so the same `Content-Encoding: gzip` header works.

#### [Bug] Errors are not handled
- **Where:** `main();`
- **Issue:** if one upload fails, you get an unhandled rejection and no clear exit code for cron.
- **Fix:** `main().catch(...)` and set `process.exitCode = 1`.

#### [High] Brotli quality 11 on data you compress every hour
- **Where:** `BROTLI_PARAM_QUALITY: 11` in `compressFile`
- **Issue:** quality 11 is made for static files that are compressed once and served many times. Your logs are new every hour, so you pay the full cost every time. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, compressing 100 MB of text used about 3019 J with brotli at quality 11, about 67 J with gzip and about 16 J with zstd ([measurements](https://metrics.green-coding.io/compare-simple.html?ids=fb08d3c3-ba41-4f0f-85fd-0b6c34e6c39b,f1479db0-c453-4aaa-9116-e137621fad07,8e93a8d2-60b0-4831-905b-764b68257bc7), [measurements](https://metrics.green-coding.io/compare-simple.html?ids=2584305c-7a1e-47e2-b6cc-26276962035e,addc5f5e-55da-47f8-956d-240d06304e4e,fb08d3c3-ba41-4f0f-85fd-0b6c34e6c39b,f1479db0-c453-4aaa-9116-e137621fad07,7b3bb87d-3b43-4f82-be99-0327405205b8,905bd408-6b31-4237-a4da-14b8fad90613,7f213a20-8028-4080-9583-6e465c217bb5,8e93a8d2-60b0-4831-905b-764b68257bc7)). That is about 45× more than gzip. This is what makes your CPU high. These numbers compare energy, not file size. Brotli files are smaller, but for logs sent inside your own network the extra size from gzip is usually not worth it.
- **Fix:** use gzip at a normal level (6). Text logs compress well even at this level.

#### [Medium] Whole file is read into memory, and compression blocks the process
- **Where:** `readFileSync` and `brotliCompressSync`
- **Issue:** a big log file is held in memory twice (raw and compressed), and nothing else can run during compression.
- **Fix:** stream the file through `createGzip()` straight into the request. No full copy in memory.

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const zlib = require('zlib');
const https = require('https');
const { pipeline } = require('stream/promises');

const LOG_DIR = '/var/log/myapp';
const UPLOAD_URL = 'https://logs.internal.example.com/ingest';

function upload(name, file) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const req = https.request(
      UPLOAD_URL,
      {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: {
          'Content-Type': 'application/octet-stream',
          'Content-Encoding': 'gzip',
          'X-File-Name': name,
        },
      },
      (res) => {
        res.resume();
        res.on('end', () => {
          if (res.statusCode === 200) resolve();
          else reject(new Error('upload failed: ' + res.statusCode));
        });
      }
    );
    req.on('error', reject);

    const source = fs.createReadStream(file);
    // .gz files are already compressed: send them as they are
    const stages = file.endsWith('.gz')
      ? [source, req]
      : [source, zlib.createGzip({ level: 6 }), req];
    pipeline(...stages).catch(reject);
  });
}

async function main() {
  const files = fs
    .readdirSync(LOG_DIR)
    .filter((f) => f.endsWith('.log') || f.endsWith('.gz'));
  for (const f of files) {
    await upload(f, path.join(LOG_DIR, f));
    console.log('uploaded', f);
  }
}

main().catch((err) => {
  console.error(err);
  process.exitCode = 1;
});
```

**Check before you deploy:** your log server must accept `Content-Encoding: gzip` on uploads. It accepts `br` now, so it likely does, but test it. If your Node version and the server both support zstd, that is cheaper again.

**Already good:** you send the logs compressed, and you do not compress twice in the same step.

