```javascript
const feed = document.getElementById("feed");
let page = 1;

window.addEventListener("scroll", async () => {
  if (window.innerHeight + window.scrollY >= document.body.offsetHeight - 200) {
    page += 1;
    const items = await fetch(`/api/feed?page=${page}`).then((r) => r.json());
    for (const item of items) {
      feed.insertAdjacentHTML("beforeend", `
        <div class="card"><div class="card-inner"><div class="card-body">
          <img src="${item.image}" alt="">
          <div class="title"><h3>${item.title}</h3></div>
          <div class="excerpt"><p>${item.excerpt}</p></div>
        </div></div></div>`);
    }
  }
});
```

**Workload:** web frontend

#### [Security] Unescaped HTML from the API
- **Where:** `${item.title}`, `${item.excerpt}`, `${item.image}` inside `insertAdjacentHTML`
- **Issue:** any HTML in a title or excerpt is injected into the page (XSS).
- **Fix:** build elements and set `textContent` / `src`, as below.

#### [High] The DOM grows without limit
- **Where:** items are only ever appended
- **Issue:** after an hour of scrolling, the page can hold thousands of cards with eight elements each, plus all their images. Layout, style recalculation and memory grow with every page, which is why phones get hot.
- **Fix:** keep a window of cards: remove cards far above the viewport (and re-fetch them if the user scrolls back up), or show a "Load more" button after a few pages. Fewer wrapper elements per card also help.
- **Pattern:** https://patterns.greensoftware.foundation/development/web-performance/avoid-excessive-dom-size

#### [Medium] Duplicate page loads from the scroll handler
- **Where:** the `scroll` listener
- **Issue:** scroll events fire many times per second. While one fetch is pending, the condition stays true and the same next page can be requested several times.
- **Fix:** use an `IntersectionObserver` on a sentinel element and a `loading` flag.

```javascript
const MAX_CARDS = 200;
const feed = document.getElementById("feed");
const sentinel = document.getElementById("feed-end");
let page = 1;
let loading = false;

function card(item) {
  const article = document.createElement("article");
  article.className = "card";
  const img = document.createElement("img");
  img.src = item.image;
  img.alt = "";
  img.loading = "lazy";
  const title = document.createElement("h3");
  title.textContent = item.title;
  const excerpt = document.createElement("p");
  excerpt.textContent = item.excerpt;
  article.append(img, title, excerpt);
  return article;
}

async function loadNextPage() {
  if (loading) return;
  loading = true;
  try {
    page += 1;
    const items = await fetch(`/api/feed?page=${page}`).then((r) => r.json());
    const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();
    items.forEach((item) => fragment.append(card(item)));
    feed.append(fragment);
    while (feed.children.length > MAX_CARDS) feed.firstElementChild.remove();
  } finally {
    loading = false;
  }
}

new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
  if (entries[0].isIntersecting) loadNextPage();
}, { rootMargin: "400px" }).observe(sentinel);
```

Removing the oldest cards changes the scroll position. Measure the removed height and adjust `scrollY`, or use CSS `overflow-anchor`. A "back to top" that reloads the first page is usually simpler than restoring removed cards.

