This version uses one `StringBuilder` and escapes the user text straight into it:

```java
import java.util.List;

public final class TestimonialHtml {

    public record Testimonial(String name, String company, String quote) {}

    private TestimonialHtml() {}

    public static String render(List<Testimonial> items) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(64 + items.size() * 256);
        sb.append("<section class=\"testimonials\">\n");
        for (Testimonial t : items) {
            sb.append("  <figure class=\"testimonial\">\n");
            sb.append("    <blockquote>");
            appendEscaped(sb, t.quote());
            sb.append("</blockquote>\n");
            sb.append("    <figcaption>");
            appendEscaped(sb, t.name());
            sb.append(", ");
            appendEscaped(sb, t.company());
            sb.append("</figcaption>\n");
            sb.append("  </figure>\n");
        }
        sb.append("</section>\n");
        return sb.toString();
    }

    private static void appendEscaped(StringBuilder sb, String s) {
        if (s == null) {
            return;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            switch (c) {
                case '&' -> sb.append("&amp;");
                case '<' -> sb.append("&lt;");
                case '>' -> sb.append("&gt;");
                case '"' -> sb.append("&quot;");
                case '\'' -> sb.append("&#39;");
                default -> sb.append(c);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Call it from your servlet with `response.getWriter().write(TestimonialHtml.render(items));`.

**Green Notes**
- **`StringBuilder` instead of `+=`** (reduces E): a `String` is immutable, so `html += ...` in a loop copies the whole text again on every step. One builder appends in place and makes one final `String`. This runs on every page view, so it adds up.
- **Escape into the same builder** (reduces E): `appendEscaped` writes to the builder directly. It does not make a temporary escaped `String` for each field, and it fixes the HTML injection risk from user quotes.
- **Start capacity:** the builder is sized from the item count, so it does not need to grow and copy its buffer many times.
- *Not applied:* `String.join` or `Collectors.joining`. They fit when you only join plain values with a separator. Here each item has a fixed HTML shape and escaping, so the builder is clearer.

