You are probably asking why `sku`, `title` and `inStock` are missing from the JSON.

`encoding/json` only sees exported fields, which are the ones that start with a capital letter. Lowercase fields are private to the package, so the encoder skips them without an error. The same is true when you decode with `json.Unmarshal`: private fields are never filled.

Export the fields and use struct tags to control the JSON names:

```go
package main

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"fmt"
	"log"
)

type Product struct {
	ID      int     `json:"id"`
	SKU     string  `json:"sku"`
	Title   string  `json:"title"`
	Price   float64 `json:"price"`
	InStock bool    `json:"in_stock"`
}

func main() {
	items := []Product{
		{ID: 1, SKU: "TS-BLK-M", Title: "T-Shirt", Price: 19.9, InStock: true},
		{ID: 2, SKU: "MG-WHT-01", Title: "Mug", Price: 8.5, InStock: false},
	}

	out, err := json.Marshal(items)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	fmt.Println(string(out))
	// [{"id":1,"sku":"TS-BLK-M","title":"T-Shirt","price":19.9,"in_stock":true},{"id":2,"sku":"MG-WHT-01","title":"Mug","price":8.5,"in_stock":false}]
}
```

Useful tag options:
- `json:"-"` keeps an exported field out of the JSON.
- `json:"note,omitempty"` leaves the field out when it has its zero value.

