Load the existing emails into a `Set` once. Then each check is a single lookup and not a scan of the whole array:

```javascript
import express from "express";
import { getAllEmails, insertSubscribers } from "./db.js";

const router = express.Router();
const MAX_EMAILS = 20000;

router.post("/subscribers/import", async (req, res) => {
  const { emails } = req.body;

  if (!Array.isArray(emails) || emails.length > MAX_EMAILS) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: `Send an "emails" array with up to ${MAX_EMAILS} items.` });
  }

  // One pass over the stored emails, built once per request.
  const known = new Set((await getAllEmails()).map((e) => e.trim().toLowerCase()));

  const toAdd = [];
  for (const raw of emails) {
    if (typeof raw !== "string") continue;
    const email = raw.trim().toLowerCase();
    if (!email.includes("@") || known.has(email)) continue;
    known.add(email); // also removes duplicates inside the uploaded file
    toAdd.push(email);
  }

  if (toAdd.length > 0) {
    await insertSubscribers(toAdd); // one call for all new emails
  }

  res.json({ added: toAdd.length, skipped: emails.length - toAdd.length });
});

export default router;
```

Your first version was probably slow because of `existing.includes(email)` (or `indexOf`) inside the loop. That scans the whole array for every uploaded email, so the work grows with uploaded emails × stored emails.

Emails are compared in lowercase, so `Anna@x.com` and `anna@x.com` count as the same person. Remove `.toLowerCase()` if your system treats them as different.

**Green Notes**
- **Set for repeated lookups** (reduces E): the Set is built once, and then thousands of checks cost O(1) each instead of O(n). This pays off because there are many lookups against one big collection.
- **Dedupe in the same pass:** `known.add(email)` also catches duplicates inside the uploaded file, with no extra loop.
- **One insert call** for all new emails, not one per email.
- *Not applied:* if the route only checked one or two emails, a plain `includes` on the array would be cheaper than building a Set. If the table grows very large, let the database do the check with a unique index on `email` and `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`, so you don't load every email into memory.

