The slow part is the `await getFraudScore(order)` inside the `for` loop. Each order waits for the previous API call to finish, so 150 orders mean 150 calls one after another. The calls do not depend on each other, so they can run in groups.

```ts
type Order = {
  id: string;
  country: string;
  postalCode: string;
  email: string;
  total: number;
};

const POSTAL_RULES: Record<string, string> = {
  DE: "^\\d{5}$",
  NL: "^\\d{4}\\s?[A-Z]{2}$",
  GB: "^[A-Z]{1,2}\\d[A-Z\\d]?\\s?\\d[A-Z]{2}$",
  US: "^\\d{5}(-\\d{4})?$",
};

// Built once when the module loads. No "g" flag, so .test() has no hidden state.
const POSTAL_PATTERNS = new Map<string, RegExp>();
for (const [country, rule] of Object.entries(POSTAL_RULES)) {
  POSTAL_PATTERNS.set(country, new RegExp(rule, "i"));
}

const MAX_PARALLEL = 10;

async function getFraudScore(order: Order): Promise<number> {
  const url =
    "https://fraud.example-shop.com/score?email=" +
    encodeURIComponent(order.email) +
    "&total=" +
    order.total;
  const res = await fetch(url);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`fraud api ${res.status}`);
  const data = await res.json();
  return data.score;
}

// Scores come back in the same order as the input orders.
async function getFraudScores(orders: Order[]): Promise<number[]> {
  const scores: number[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < orders.length; i += MAX_PARALLEL) {
    const group = orders.slice(i, i + MAX_PARALLEL);
    scores.push(...(await Promise.all(group.map(getFraudScore))));
  }
  return scores;
}

export async function checkNewOrders(orders: Order[]) {
  // Step 1: cheap postal check for every order, no network.
  const reasons: (string | null)[] = orders.map((order) => {
    const re = POSTAL_PATTERNS.get(order.country);
    return re && !re.test(order.postalCode.trim()) ? "bad postal code" : null;
  });

  // Step 2: fraud score only for orders that passed step 1, in parallel groups.
  const toScore = orders.filter((_, i) => reasons[i] === null);
  const scores = await getFraudScores(toScore);

  // Step 3: build the result in the original order.
  const accepted: Order[] = [];
  const rejected: { id: string; reason: string }[] = [];
  let next = 0; // position in `scores`, only moves for orders that were scored
  orders.forEach((order, i) => {
    let reason = reasons[i];
    if (reason === null && scores[next++] > 0.8) reason = "fraud score";
    if (reason === null) accepted.push(order);
    else rejected.push({ id: order.id, reason });
  });

  return { accepted, rejected };
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **Parallel fraud calls with `Promise.all`** (reduces E, network wait): the loop held the function open for 150 round trips in a row. Now at most 10 are in flight at once. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks (Python, 10 HTTP requests), async used about 7% less CPU energy and about 17% less wall time than sync, so for a small batch the gain is mostly time, not energy ([measurements](https://metrics.green-coding.io/compare-simple.html?ids=97acc9a0-3856-4bbb-b816-2b64030fba98,6415cccb-ad1a-4d87-ae46-92740c5e285b)). The cap of 10 keeps you friendly to the fraud API's rate limit. Raise it only if the API allows it.
- **Regex built once** (reduces E): `new RegExp(rule, "i")` ran for every order, but the rules never change. They are now compiled when the file loads. The gain is small, because JavaScript engines cache compiled patterns. It costs nothing, and it matters more if the rules ever get built from changing input.
- **Same results as before:** `accepted` and `rejected` keep the order of the input, and orders with a bad postal code still skip the API call. One difference: if the fraud API fails, the function still throws, but requests of the current group of 10 may already be in flight.
- *Not applied:* no batch endpoint or caching of scores. A fraud score depends on email and total, so I would not reuse old scores without knowing how the API treats them.

Can you review this enrichment step of our nightly order ETL? Requirements and context:

- About 30 million order lines per run, read from Parquet and processed in one JVM.
- Each line has a `categoryId`. We add the full category path ("Home > Garden > Tools") for the BI export.
- The category tree has roughly 4,000 nodes. It is loaded once at startup and never changes during a run.
- Output must stay identical, same order as the input. Unknown category ids must still give "UNKNOWN".
- I added a small cache last week because the step was slow, but the run time did not improve as much as I hoped. Sometimes the row count of the output is also off by a few rows, which I can't explain.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class OrderLineEnricher {

    public record OrderLine(long orderId, String sku, long amountCents, int categoryId) {}

    public record EnrichedLine(long orderId, String sku, long amountCents, String categoryPath) {}

    private final Map<Integer, Integer> parentOf;
    private final Map<Integer, String> nameOf;
    private final Map<Integer, String> pathCache = new HashMap<>();

    public OrderLineEnricher(Map<Integer, Integer> parentOf, Map<Integer, String> nameOf) {
        this.parentOf = parentOf;
        this.nameOf = nameOf;
    }

    private String categoryPath(int categoryId) {
        if (pathCache.containsKey(categoryId)) {
            return pathCache.get(categoryId);
        }
        Deque<String> parts = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Integer current = categoryId;
        while (current != null) {
            String name = nameOf.get(current);
            if (name == null) {
                return "UNKNOWN";
            }
            parts.addFirst(name);
            current = parentOf.get(current);
        }
        String path = String.join(" > ", parts);
        pathCache.put(categoryId, path);
        return path;
    }

    public List<EnrichedLine> enrich(List<OrderLine> lines) {
        return lines.parallelStream()
                .map(l -> new EnrichedLine(l.orderId(), l.sku(), l.amountCents(),
                        categoryPath(l.categoryId())))
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```

**Workload:** data pipeline (per-row work, 30 million rows per run)

#### [Bug] Plain `HashMap` written from a parallel stream
- **Where:** `pathCache` and `categoryPath()`, called from `parallelStream()`
- **Issue:** `HashMap` is not thread-safe. Several worker threads call `put` at the same time, especially early in the run when the cache is empty. This can lose entries, corrupt the table, or return wrong values. It can also cause an endless loop in older JVMs. Your output row count is fixed by the stream, so the "few rows off" is most likely a symptom of the same race, for example a wrong path on some rows or a worker that failed. Treat it as a correctness bug, not noise.
- **Fix:** use a `ConcurrentHashMap`.
- **Impact:** correctness first. It also stops threads from wasting work on a damaged map.

#### [Medium] Cache is the right idea, but it is used in a costly way
- **Where:** `containsKey` followed by `get`, then `put` after the build
- **Issue:** the memoization fits your case well. The tree is fixed for the whole run and there are about 4,000 distinct ids against 30 million lookups, so each path should be built about 4,000 times instead of once per row. But a hit needs two hash lookups, and many threads can build the same path at once on a cold cache. That explains why the gain is smaller than expected.
- **Fix:** read once with `get`. On a miss, use `computeIfAbsent` with a builder that does not touch the cache itself. This is safe because the builder is not recursive. `computeIfAbsent` stores nothing when the builder returns `null`, so unknown ids are still not cached.
- **Impact:** reduces E per row. After warm-up, a row costs one map read instead of a tree walk, a `Deque` allocation and a string join.

#### [Low] Cache validity depends on an unwritten rule
- **Where:** the class as a whole
- **Issue:** the cache is only correct because `parentOf` and `nameOf` never change after construction. The cache is also unbounded, which is fine here because it can hold at most one entry per known category.
- **Fix:** add a short comment, and if the tree is reloaded, create a new enricher instead of mutating the maps. Don't memoize if the tree could change during a run.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class OrderLineEnricher {

    public record OrderLine(long orderId, String sku, long amountCents, int categoryId) {}

    public record EnrichedLine(long orderId, String sku, long amountCents, String categoryPath) {}

    // parentOf and nameOf must not change after construction: paths are memoized.
    private final Map<Integer, Integer> parentOf;
    private final Map<Integer, String> nameOf;
    private final Map<Integer, String> pathCache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public OrderLineEnricher(Map<Integer, Integer> parentOf, Map<Integer, String> nameOf) {
        this.parentOf = parentOf;
        this.nameOf = nameOf;
    }

    // Returns null for unknown ids, so they are never cached.
    private String buildPath(int categoryId) {
        Deque<String> parts = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Integer current = categoryId;
        while (current != null) {
            String name = nameOf.get(current);
            if (name == null) {
                return null;
            }
            parts.addFirst(name);
            current = parentOf.get(current);
        }
        return String.join(" > ", parts);
    }

    private String categoryPath(int categoryId) {
        String cached = pathCache.get(categoryId);
        if (cached != null) {
            return cached;
        }
        String path = pathCache.computeIfAbsent(categoryId, this::buildPath);
        return path != null ? path : "UNKNOWN";
    }

    public List<EnrichedLine> enrich(List<OrderLine> lines) {
        return lines.parallelStream()
                .map(l -> new EnrichedLine(l.orderId(), l.sku(), l.amountCents(),
                        categoryPath(l.categoryId())))
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```

**Already good:** the cache is keyed by the small id, unknown ids are not stored, and `collect(Collectors.toList())` on an ordered stream keeps the input order.

