```go
package etl

import (
	"strconv"
	"strings"
)

type Record struct {
	ID     int64
	Source string
	Value  float64
	Tags   []string
}

// BuildCSV makes the payload we bulk load into the warehouse.
func BuildCSV(records []Record) string {
	const header = "id,source,value,tags\n"

	var b strings.Builder
	b.Grow(len(header) + len(records)*48) // rough size guess, avoids most regrowth
	b.WriteString(header)

	for _, r := range records {
		if r.Source == "" {
			continue
		}
		b.WriteString(strconv.FormatInt(r.ID, 10))
		b.WriteByte(',')
		b.WriteString(r.Source)
		b.WriteByte(',')
		b.WriteString(strconv.FormatFloat(r.Value, 'f', 3, 64))
		b.WriteByte(',')
		b.WriteString(strings.Join(r.Tags, "|"))
		b.WriteByte('\n')
	}
	return b.String()
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **`strings.Builder` instead of `out += line`** (reduces E): Go strings are immutable, so every `+=` allocates a new string and copies everything written so far. For n records the bytes copied grow roughly with n², and the garbage collector has to clean up all the old copies. The builder appends into one buffer, so total copying grows linearly.
- **`strings.Join` for the tags:** the inner `tags +=` loop did the same copying per record. `Join` sizes the result once.
- **No `fmt.Sprintf` per row:** `strconv.FormatFloat(v, 'f', 3, 64)` gives the same text as `%.3f` without format parsing and reflection.
- Output is byte for byte the same as before. A rough `Grow` guess is enough, the builder still grows if it is too small.
- *Not applied:* streaming straight to an `io.Writer` (for example `bufio.Writer` or `csv.Writer`). It would avoid holding the whole payload in memory, but it changes the function signature, so only do it if the loader accepts a reader.

Show the results page by page, and only put the current page into the DOM:

```javascript
const PAGE_SIZE = 50;
const list = document.getElementById("results");
const moreButton = document.getElementById("more");
const priceFormat = new Intl.NumberFormat("en-US", { style: "currency", currency: "USD" });

let results = [];
let shown = 0;

function renderNextPage() {
  const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();
  for (const p of results.slice(shown, shown + PAGE_SIZE)) {
    const li = document.createElement("li");
    const link = document.createElement("a");
    link.href = p.url;
    link.textContent = `${p.name} – ${priceFormat.format(p.price)}`;
    li.append(link);
    fragment.append(li);
  }
  list.append(fragment);
  shown += PAGE_SIZE;
  moreButton.hidden = shown >= results.length;
}

export function showResults(products) {
  results = products;
  shown = 0;
  list.replaceChildren();
  renderNextPage();
}

moreButton.addEventListener("click", renderNextPage);
```

```html
<ul id="results"></ul>
<button id="more" hidden>Show more results</button>
```

**Green Notes**
- **Small DOM** (reduces E on the client): 50 list items at a time instead of up to 5000. Every DOM node costs memory, style calculation and layout, and most users never scroll past the first results.
  https://patterns.greensoftware.foundation/development/web-performance/avoid-excessive-dom-size
