The slow part is `Source.valueOf(...)`. It throws an exception for every unknown value, and many ad links come from sources that are not in your enum (bing, reddit, blogs). Throwing builds a stack trace each time, and a servlet stack is deep. This is normal input, not an error, so it should not use an exception.

```java
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import java.util.EnumMap;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Locale;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.LongAdder;

public class LandingTracker {

    public enum Source { GOOGLE, FACEBOOK, NEWSLETTER, TWITTER, LINKEDIN, PARTNER, DIRECT }

    private static final Map<String, Source> BY_NAME = new HashMap<>();
    static {
        for (Source s : Source.values()) {
            BY_NAME.put(s.name(), s);
        }
    }

    private final Map<Source, LongAdder> visits = new EnumMap<>(Source.class);

    public LandingTracker() {
        for (Source s : Source.values()) {
            visits.put(s, new LongAdder());
        }
    }

    public Source detectSource(HttpServletRequest req) {
        String raw = req.getParameter("utm_source");
        if (raw == null) {
            return Source.DIRECT;
        }
        return BY_NAME.getOrDefault(raw.trim().toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT), Source.DIRECT);
    }

    public void recordVisit(HttpServletRequest req) {
        visits.get(detectSource(req)).increment();
    }

    public String heroTitle(Source s) {
        switch (s) {
            case NEWSLETTER: return "Welcome back, reader!";
            case PARTNER:    return "Hello from our partner network";
            default:         return "Try it free for 30 days";
        }
    }

    public Map<Source, Long> snapshot() {
        Map<Source, Long> copy = new EnumMap<>(Source.class);
        visits.forEach((s, n) -> copy.put(s, n.sum()));
        return copy;
    }
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **No exception for expected input** (reduces E): unknown `utm_source` values now cost one hash lookup instead of a thrown and caught `IllegalArgumentException` with a full stack trace. The more ad traffic comes from unlisted sources, the more this saves, and it is paid on every request. Results are the same: unknown, empty and missing values all give `DIRECT`, and matching is still case-insensitive after `trim()`.
- **Bug fix:** `visits` was a plain `EnumMap` changed by many request threads at once, so counts could be lost or corrupted. Each source now has a `LongAdder`, created once in the constructor. The map itself is never changed after that, so it is safe to read from many threads.
- **API change:** `snapshot()` now returns `Map<Source, Long>` and includes sources with count 0. If other code needs `Integer` or only non-zero entries, filter or convert there.
- *Not applied:* no cache or extra layer for the lookup. A map of 7 entries is already as cheap as it gets.

Please review this class from our CI maintenance job (Java 17). It runs every night, loops over all ~400 repositories in our internal container registry, and deletes old build tags so the registry doesn't fill up.

Requirements:
- Tags that count as builds are `build-123` and plain `123` (old pipeline).
- Everything else must never be deleted: `latest`, `v1.4.0`, `stable`, commit SHAs like `a1b2c3d`.
- Keep the 20 newest builds per repo, delete the rest.

Some repos have several thousand tags, and most of them are not build tags. I want feedback on correctness and on how the code behaves at this size.

```java
public class TagCleaner {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(TagCleaner.class);
    private static final int KEEP_LAST = 20;

    private final RegistryClient registry;

    public TagCleaner(RegistryClient registry) {
        this.registry = registry;
    }

    public List<String> selectTagsToDelete(String repo) {
        List<String> tags = registry.listTags(repo);
        TreeMap<Integer, String> builds = new TreeMap<>();
        for (String tag : tags) {
            String num = tag.startsWith("build-") ? tag.substring("build-".length()) : tag;
            try {
                builds.put(Integer.parseInt(num), tag);
            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
                log.debug("Not a build tag: {}", tag, e);
            }
        }

        List<String> toDelete = new ArrayList<>();
        int excess = builds.size() - KEEP_LAST;
        for (String tag : builds.values()) {
            if (excess-- <= 0) {
                break;
            }
            toDelete.add(tag);
        }
        return toDelete;
    }

    public void run(String repo) {
        for (String tag : selectTagsToDelete(repo)) {
            registry.deleteTag(repo, tag);
        }
    }
}
```

**Workload:** CI/CD maintenance job (scheduled batch)

#### [Bug] Digit-only commit SHAs are treated as build numbers
- **Where:** `Integer.parseInt(num)` on tags without a `build-` prefix
- **Issue:** a short SHA like `1234567` is all digits, so it parses as build number 1,234,567. It sorts as the newest "build" and pushes real builds out of the keep window. Those builds then get deleted. Seven-digit SHAs that happen to be all digits are rare, but across thousands of tags it will happen.
- **Fix:** only accept a bounded number of digits for bare numbers. SHAs have 7 or more characters, so I assumed old-pipeline numbers have at most 6 digits. Please check that against your registry.

#### [Bug] `build-42` and `42` overwrite each other
- **Where:** `builds.put(Integer.parseInt(num), tag)`
- **Issue:** both tags map to key `42`. The later one replaces the earlier one in the map, so the other tag is never a deletion candidate and stays in the registry forever.
- **Fix:** map each number to a list of tags, and count builds by number.

#### [Medium] Exceptions used to skip non-build tags
- **Where:** `try { Integer.parseInt(...) } catch (NumberFormatException e)`
- **Issue:** most tags are not build tags (`latest`, `v1.4.0`, SHAs). Each one creates an exception, and that includes filling in the stack trace. `log.debug(..., e)` also formats the full stack trace whenever debug logging is on. This happens for most tags in all ~400 repos every night. `parseInt` also accepts `+5`, `-3` and non-ASCII digits, which you do not want as build numbers.
- **Fix:** check the characters first with a small helper that returns `-1` for "not a build tag". Nothing is thrown on the normal path. Log the skipped tag without the exception.
- **Impact:** reduces E in the nightly job: no allocation or stack walk per skipped tag, only a short character scan. The gain is bounded, because the job runs once a day, so this is not urgent. The fix is small and makes the intent clearer.

```java
public class TagCleaner {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(TagCleaner.class);
    private static final int KEEP_LAST = 20;
    private static final String PREFIX = "build-";
    private static final int MAX_DIGITS_PREFIXED = 9;  // fits in int
    private static final int MAX_DIGITS_BARE = 6;      // SHAs have 7+ chars

    private final RegistryClient registry;

    public TagCleaner(RegistryClient registry) {
        this.registry = registry;
    }

    /** Returns the build number, or -1 if the tag is not a build tag. */
    static int parseBuildNumber(String tag) {
        boolean prefixed = tag.startsWith(PREFIX);
        int start = prefixed ? PREFIX.length() : 0;
        int digits = tag.length() - start;
        int max = prefixed ? MAX_DIGITS_PREFIXED : MAX_DIGITS_BARE;
        if (digits < 1 || digits > max) {
            return -1;
        }
        int n = 0;
        for (int i = start; i < tag.length(); i++) {
            char c = tag.charAt(i);
            if (c < '0' || c > '9') {
                return -1;
            }
            n = n * 10 + (c - '0');
        }
        return n;
    }

    public List<String> selectTagsToDelete(String repo) {
        TreeMap<Integer, List<String>> builds = new TreeMap<>();
        for (String tag : registry.listTags(repo)) {
            int n = parseBuildNumber(tag);
            if (n < 0) {
                log.debug("Not a build tag: {}", tag);
                continue;
            }
            builds.computeIfAbsent(n, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(tag);
        }

        List<String> toDelete = new ArrayList<>();
        int excess = builds.size() - KEEP_LAST;  // counts distinct build numbers
        for (List<String> sameBuild : builds.values()) {
            if (excess-- <= 0) {
                break;
            }
            toDelete.addAll(sameBuild);
        }
        return toDelete;
    }

    public void run(String repo) {
        for (String tag : selectTagsToDelete(repo)) {
            registry.deleteTag(repo, tag);
        }
    }
}
```

Behavior changes to be aware of: bare numbers with more than 6 digits and prefixed numbers with more than 9 digits are no longer treated as builds. A build is now kept or deleted together with all tags that share its number.

**Already good:** a `TreeMap` gives the oldest-first order directly, so there is no extra sort. `selectTagsToDelete` is separate from the deletion, which makes a dry run easy.

