```java
@Service
public class ProductLookupService {

    private final ProductRepository repo;
    private final Metrics metrics;

    public ProductLookupService(ProductRepository repo, Metrics metrics) {
        this.repo = repo;
        this.metrics = metrics;
    }

    // Plain ASCII digits, at most 18 of them: always fits in a long.
    private static boolean looksLikeId(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        if (n == 0 || n > 18) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            if (c < '0' || c > '9') {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    // GET /products/{key}  (key is a numeric id or a slug like "blue-running-shoes")
    public Product resolve(String key) {
        metrics.count("product.resolve");
        Optional<Product> found = looksLikeId(key)
                ? repo.findById(Long.parseLong(key))
                : repo.findBySlug(key);
        return found.orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException("product " + key));
    }

    // GET /products?ids=12,15,abc,98
    public List<Product> resolveMany(String idsParam) {
        List<Long> ids = new ArrayList<>();
        for (String part : idsParam.split(",")) {
            String p = part.trim();
            if (looksLikeId(p)) {
                ids.add(Long.parseLong(p));
            }
        }
        return repo.findAllById(ids);
    }
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **No exceptions for expected input** (reduces E): every slug request used to throw a `NumberFormatException` just to reach the slug branch. Creating an exception captures a full stack trace, and in a servlet or Spring stack that trace is deep. That cost was paid on every slug request and on every bad id in `ids=`. A short character check now decides the branch, and `parseLong` only runs on input that is known to be valid, so it never throws.
- **Behavior change to be aware of:** only plain ASCII digits count as an id now. Before, `+5`, `-5` and non-ASCII digits were also accepted by `parseLong`. Strings of 19 or more digits are now treated as slugs and are not parsed as ids. Product ids are positive and well below that length, so real lookups give the same result. If you need signed ids, allow one leading sign in `looksLikeId`.
- **Left as is:** the `NotFoundException` path. A missing product is an error response, and it is not the common case.
- I did not add any vectorized or JIT-style numeric code. Nothing here is a numeric hot loop, and parsing a few ids per request is not a CPU-bound path.

