**Green Notes**
- **CSS keyframes, no JavaScript timer** (reduces E): the bar and the dot are animated only with `transform` and `opacity` in `@keyframes`. The browser runs this without calling your JS on every frame. In Green Metrics Tool measurements of a 20-second animation in a real browser, `requestAnimationFrame` with style updates used about 161 J CPU energy and CSS `@keyframes` about 145 J (about 10% less) ([measurements](https://metrics.green-coding.io/compare-simple.html?ids=4a2085fd-6f43-4f26-a3ee-2a6fcd7db594,58ee50a2-7110-4418-beea-df6ffba5425a)).
- **Animation runs only in the `running` state:** when the job is done or failed, the bar and dot are static. Nothing keeps animating on a tab that stays open for hours.
- **`prefers-reduced-motion`** turns the animation off for users who ask for it. This also saves the work.

The bar has no real percentage, because the API only gives `state`. If the job can report progress, set `width` on the bar from that value instead of the sliding animation.

```rust
/// `ts` must be sorted ascending. Window is [start, start + width).
pub fn busiest_window(ts: &[u64], width: u64) -> Option<(u64, usize)> {
    if ts.is_empty() || width == 0 {
        return None;
    }
    let mut left = 0;
    let mut best = (ts[0], 1usize);
    for right in 0..ts.len() {
        while ts[right] - ts[left] >= width {
            left += 1;
        }
        let count = right - left + 1;
        if count > best.1 {
            best = (ts[left], count);
        }
    }
    Some(best)
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn finds_earliest_busiest() {
        let ts = [1, 2, 3, 10, 11, 12, 30];
        assert_eq!(busiest_window(&ts, 5), Some((1, 3)));
        assert_eq!(busiest_window(&ts, 1), Some((1, 1)));
        assert_eq!(busiest_window(&[], 5), None);
    }
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **Two pointers instead of rescanning** (reduces E): both indexes only move forward, so the work is O(n). Counting the events inside a window for every start timestamp would be O(n²) on a large log.
- **No allocation:** it works on the borrowed slice and keeps only a few integers, so memory stays flat for big inputs.
- Sorted input is required. If your lines can arrive out of order, sort first (O(n log n)), which is still far cheaper than the quadratic scan.

