```javascript
const availableWishes = (wishlist, inStock) => {
  const stock = new Set(inStock);
  return wishlist.filter((id) => stock.has(id));
};
```

**Green Notes**
- **Set lookup instead of nested search** (reduces E): `inStock.includes(id)` inside the filter would be up to 20k × 20k comparisons. With a set, each id is checked in O(1), so the whole function is linear.
- Keeps the wishlist order, which is what users see.

```javascript
function availableWishes(wishlist, inStock) {
  // Put the stock ids into a Set once, so each lookup below is fast.
  const stockIds = new Set(inStock);

  const result = [];
  for (const productId of wishlist) {
    if (stockIds.has(productId)) {
      result.push(productId);
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

Same algorithm: one `Set` built once, one pass over the wishlist. The loop version is just as efficient as `filter`. The important part is the `Set`, so the comment explains why it is there, so nobody "simplifies" it back to `includes` later.

Load the IDs once into a `HashSet`. Each check is then one hash lookup, no matter how many devices are registered.

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class DeviceFilter {

    public record SensorReading(String deviceId, long timestampMs, double value) {}

    private final Set<String> registered;

    public DeviceFilter(Path registryFile) throws IOException {
        try (var lines = Files.lines(registryFile)) {
            this.registered = lines
                    .map(String::trim)
                    .filter(line -> !line.isEmpty() && !line.startsWith("#"))
                    .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableSet());
        }
    }

    public boolean isRegistered(String deviceId) {
        return registered.contains(deviceId);
    }

    public List<SensorReading> keepRegistered(List<SensorReading> batch) {
        return batch.stream()
                .filter(reading -> registered.contains(reading.deviceId()))
                .toList();
    }
}
```

Usage:

```java
// once, at startup
DeviceFilter filter = new DeviceFilter(Path.of("registered_devices.txt"));

// for every incoming batch
List<DeviceFilter.SensorReading> clean = filter.keepRegistered(batch);
```

Create the filter once and reuse it. If you build it per batch, you read the file again every time. The set is read-only, so several threads can use it safely. If you add devices while the gateway runs, build a new `DeviceFilter` and swap the reference.

**Green Notes**
- **Set for repeated membership tests** (reduces E): with a `List`, every reading scans up to 3000 IDs. With a `HashSet`, it is one lookup. The gateway checks every reading, so the saving repeats on every batch and matters on a small CPU.
- **Registry loaded once:** the file is read and parsed at startup, not per batch.
- *Not applied:* a set only pays off with repeated lookups. If you checked one ID once, a plain list scan would be cheaper than building the set.

