```java
import java.util.Collection;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public final class ChangedFileFilter {

    private ChangedFileFilter() {}

    public static List<String> filesToBuild(List<String> changed, Collection<String> ignored) {
        Set<String> skip = new HashSet<>(ignored);
        return changed.stream()
                .filter(path -> !skip.contains(path))
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```

The result keeps the order of `changed`. Matching is by exact path. If `.ciignore` has glob patterns, you need a different matcher.

**Green Notes**
- **Set for repeated membership tests** (reduces E): the ignore list is copied into a `HashSet` once. Each changed file then costs one hash lookup instead of a scan over thousands of entries. The work goes from O(changed × ignored) to O(changed + ignored). This runs on every pipeline, so the saving repeats.
- **Order is kept** because the set is only used for lookups, and the stream walks `changed` in its original order.
- *Not applied:* if you only check one path against the list, keep the list. Building the set costs more than one scan.

